{"object_id":"iteration_01","revision":1,"content_hash":"2e59c9980e0c8f16c6e691c8267e943a88822849472f8f3b660d4b3eca0971ee","source_run_id":"cloud-repair-formal-ar-04","input_refs":[],"unconsumed_metadata":{},"schema_version":"observation.v2","acquisition_id":"iteration_01","candidate_ref":null,"objective_ref":null,"sample_ref":null,"binding":{},"raw_blocks":[],"derived_features":{"legacy_projection":{"iteration_id":"iteration_01","route_id":"route_01","route_title":"Optimization of SiO2/TiO2 Bilayer Order and Thickness for Broadband AR","route_snapshot":{"route_id":"route_01","title":"Optimization of SiO2/TiO2 Bilayer Order and Thickness for Broadband AR","route_kind":"optimize_existing_stack","scientific_hypothesis":"For a two-layer system with indices n_air < n_SiO2 < n_TiO2 > n_glass, the stack ordering Air/SiO2/TiO2/Glass yields a lower global minimum mean reflectance than Air/TiO2/SiO2/Glass because the intermediate SiO2 layer reduces the initial impedance mismatch at the air interface more effectively than starting with the high-index TiO2.","design_principle":"Impedance matching in multilayer optics suggests that arranging layers in ascending order of refractive index (or close to it) minimizes Fresnel reflections at each interface. Placing the lower index SiO2 (n=1.46) adjacent to air (n=1.0) creates a smaller step change than placing TiO2 (n=2.40) adjacent to air, potentially broadening the low-reflectance bandwidth despite the non-ideal final step from TiO2 to Glass (n=1.52).","proposed_materials":["SiO2 (Gao)","TiO2 (Siefke)"],"proposed_topology":"Air (semi-infinite) / SiO2 (finite) / TiO2 (finite) / Glass (semi-infinite)","design_variables":["thickness of SiO2 layer (continuous, 40-160 nm)","thickness of TiO2 layer (continuous, 40-160 nm)"],"soft_objectives":["minimize arithmetic mean power reflectance (s-pol, normal incidence) over 450-700 nm","achieve mean reflectance below 0.03 as a preference"],"manufacturing_considerations":["layer thicknesses must remain within 40 nm to 160 nm bounds","exactly one layer of each material permitted"],"evidence_ids":[],"theory_basis":["Fresnel equations for normal incidence reflection at dielectric interfaces","Transfer Matrix Method (TMM) for calculating coherent superposition of fields in stratified media","Quarter-wave transformer theory generalized for broadband minimization via numerical optimization"],"expected_advantages":["Directly tests the theoretically preferred material ordering for impedance grading","Concentrates the limited 64-evaluation budget on refining thicknesses for the most promising topology rather than splitting resources between two topologies","Provides a clear baseline; if this optimal ordering fails to meet the target, the reverse ordering is unlikely to succeed given the larger initial index mismatch"],"known_risks":["The reverse order (TiO2/SiO2) might unexpectedly perform better due to specific phase cancellation effects at the substrate interface not captured by simple impedance arguments","The thickness constraints (40-160 nm) may prevent the formation of ideal quarter-wave or half-wave conditions for the entire 450-700 nm band","Constant index assumption may introduce errors if actual material dispersion is significant, though the problem statement mandates constant indices"],"execution_request_english":"Execute a deterministic Transfer Matrix Method (TMM) optimization for a planar four-medium stack: Air (n=1.0), Layer 1 (SiO2 (Gao), n=1.46), Layer 2 (TiO2 (Siefke), n=2.40), Substrate (Glass, n=1.52). The optimization must vary the thickness of the SiO2 (Gao) layer and the TiO2 (Siefke) layer independently, subject to hard bounds of 40 nm to 160 nm for each. The objective function is the arithmetic mean of the power reflectance for s-polarized light at normal incidence (0 degrees), calculated at 5 nm intervals from 450 nm to 700 nm inclusive. Use a deterministic optimizer with seed 17, allowing a maximum of 64 solver evaluations. Report the best candidate's thicknesses, the full reflectance spectrum, and the calculated mean reflectance. Do not use RCWA, FDFD, or surrogate models.","priority":1,"parent_route_id":null,"revision_reason":null},"compilation_status":"compiled","compilation_rationale":"User requests constant-index layers and s-polarization at normal incidence. Fixed order SiO2/TiO2 declared for Experiment 1. Thickness bounds 40-160 nm applied. Mean reflectance minimization mapped to objective. Seed 17 and eval limit noted in rationale as execution constraints, not task parameters.","compilation_errors":[],"run_status":"completed","physically_valid_candidate_count":5,"best_target_score":0.9646375913435794,"best_robustness_score":0.5887842007325347,"selected_candidate_ids":["ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__01","ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__02","ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__04"],"failure_categories":[],"experiment_summaries":[{"experiment_id":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt","mode":"optimize","physically_valid_candidate_count":5,"best_target_score":0.9646375913435794,"selected_roles":{"best_target_score":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__01","most_robust":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__01","simplest_fabrication":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__02","structurally_distinctive":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__04"}}],"candidate_summaries":[{"candidate_id":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt__baseline","experiment_id":"ar_coating_sio2_tio2_opt","target_score":0.9324000766171716,"robustness_score":null,"simplicity_score":1.0,"distinctiveness_score":0.09366077406978925,"thicknesses_nm":[80.0,80.0],"optimizer_id":null,"objective_report":{"schema_version":"tmm-objective-report.v1","aggregate_soft_score":0.42517034502848056,"weighted_directional_loss":0.005703226405832365,"target_attainment":{"min_mean_reflectance":{"metric":"mean_reflectance","observed":0.06759992338282833,"target":0.0,"sense":"minimize","weight":1.0,"s